WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.874
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.174 --> 00:00:08.235
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.535 --> 00:00:16.993
Unit twenty: Gender and Equality. In this unit, you will discuss gender equality at work and in society.

4
00:00:17.983 --> 00:00:19.066
gender gap.

5
00:00:19.766 --> 00:00:24.459
gender gap means a difference between men and women in opportunities or outcomes.

6
00:00:24.759 --> 00:00:27.467
The gender gap in science careers is narrowing.

7
00:00:28.167 --> 00:00:30.807
Your turn. Say: gender gap.

8
00:00:33.507 --> 00:00:34.473
gender gap.

9
00:00:35.483 --> 00:00:36.589
pay equity.

10
00:00:37.289 --> 00:00:40.658
pay equity means equal pay for work of equal value.

11
00:00:40.958 --> 00:00:43.713
The company published a report on pay equity.

12
00:00:44.413 --> 00:00:46.972
Your turn. Say: pay equity.

13
00:00:49.672 --> 00:00:50.615
pay equity.

14
00:00:51.617 --> 00:00:52.676
glass ceiling.

15
00:00:53.376 --> 00:00:58.475
glass ceiling means an invisible barrier that stops women reaching top positions.

16
00:00:58.775 --> 00:01:01.634
She broke the glass ceiling by becoming CEO.

17
00:01:02.334 --> 00:01:05.474
Your turn. Say: glass ceiling.

18
00:01:08.174 --> 00:01:09.233
glass ceiling.

19
00:01:10.217 --> 00:01:11.624
representation.

20
00:01:12.324 --> 00:01:16.530
representation means the presence of a group in important roles.

21
00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:20.571
Women's representation in parliament is increasing.

22
00:01:21.271 --> 00:01:24.875
Your turn. Say: representation.

23
00:01:27.575 --> 00:01:28.831
representation.

24
00:01:29.817 --> 00:01:30.957
gender norm.

25
00:01:31.657 --> 00:01:36.455
gender norm means social expectations about how men and women should behave.

26
00:01:36.755 --> 00:01:40.124
Gender norms influence career choices from childhood.

27
00:01:40.824 --> 00:01:43.604
Your turn. Say: gender norm.

28
00:01:46.304 --> 00:01:47.874
gender norm.

29
00:01:48.883 --> 00:01:50.349
empowerment programme.

30
00:01:51.049 --> 00:01:55.510
empowerment programme means a programme that gives people skills and confidence.

31
00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:58.854
The empowerment programme trained women entrepreneurs.

32
00:01:59.554 --> 00:02:02.729
Your turn. Say: empowerment programme.

33
00:02:05.429 --> 00:02:07.010
empowerment programme.

34
00:02:08.017 --> 00:02:09.621
unconscious bias.

35
00:02:10.321 --> 00:02:14.364
unconscious bias means prejudice we have without realising it.

36
00:02:14.664 --> 00:02:17.871
Training can reduce unconscious bias in hiring.

37
00:02:18.571 --> 00:02:22.141
Your turn. Say: unconscious bias.

38
00:02:24.841 --> 00:02:26.434
unconscious bias.

39
00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:29.241
work-life policy.

40
00:02:29.941 --> 00:02:35.330
work-life policy means workplace rules that support family and personal life.

41
00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:39.859
A strong work-life policy helps parents stay in their jobs.

42
00:02:40.559 --> 00:02:44.361
Your turn. Say: work-life policy.

43
00:02:47.061 --> 00:02:49.095
work-life policy.

44
00:02:50.083 --> 00:02:52.245
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:52.545 --> 00:02:56.054
Ms. Sarah, is the gender gap still a serious issue?

46
00:02:57.350 --> 00:03:02.464
In many sectors, yes. Women remain under-represented in senior positions.

47
00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:06.330
Is that because of the glass ceiling?

48
00:03:07.617 --> 00:03:13.427
Partly. Unconscious bias in promotion decisions also plays a role.

49
00:03:14.717 --> 00:03:19.595
My sister says her boss always gives the big projects to the guys.

50
00:03:20.883 --> 00:03:26.380
That's a common complaint. Formally, we'd call it unequal access to opportunities.

51
00:03:27.683 --> 00:03:29.172
What could companies do?

52
00:03:30.483 --> 00:03:35.873
Introduce transparent pay equity reviews and better work-life policies, for a start.

53
00:03:37.183 --> 00:03:39.810
Grammar focus: Register and tone.

54
00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:43.700
Register is the level of formality in your language.

55
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:53.026
Informally, we might say the boss gives the big projects to the guys. Formally, male employees receive greater access to high-profile projects.

56
00:03:53.326 --> 00:03:55.953
Choose your register to suit your audience.

57
00:03:56.253 --> 00:04:04.742
At C1, choose your register deliberately: informal with friends, formal in reports, presentations and emails to managers.

58
00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:08.251
Now read and listen. Closing the Gap.

59
00:04:08.551 --> 00:04:15.218
Over the past fifty years, women have made significant progress in education and employment.

60
00:04:16.517 --> 00:04:21.163
Nevertheless, a considerable gender gap persists in pay and leadership.

61
00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:27.642
Women remain under-represented in senior management and political office.

62
00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:35.640
Research suggests that unconscious bias influences recruitment and promotion decisions.

63
00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:41.806
Traditional gender norms also shape expectations about caregiving and careers.

64
00:04:43.117 --> 00:04:48.959
Organisations that implement transparent pay equity reviews tend to reduce disparities.

65
00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:56.104
Empowerment programmes and flexible work-life policies have proved particularly effective.

66
00:04:57.417 --> 00:05:04.107
Equality, it is increasingly recognised, benefits not only women but entire economies.

67
00:05:05.417 --> 00:05:08.266
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:08.566 --> 00:05:12.609
An invisible barrier stopping women reaching top jobs is the blank.

69
00:05:12.909 --> 00:05:17.759
A, glass ceiling. B, gender norm. C, pay equity.

70
00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:24.168
The answer is A: glass ceiling.

71
00:05:25.683 --> 00:05:27.160
Which is the most formal?

72
00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:33.923
A, It's a big deal.. B, It is a significant issue.. C, It's kind of important..

73
00:05:37.917 --> 00:05:41.263
The answer is B: It is a significant issue..

74
00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:44.088
Which is informal?

75
00:05:44.388 --> 00:05:49.121
A, male employees. B, the guys. C, male colleagues.

76
00:05:53.117 --> 00:05:55.975
The answer is B: the guys.

77
00:05:57.483 --> 00:06:00.272
Prejudice we have without realising it is blank.

78
00:06:00.572 --> 00:06:06.002
A, representation. B, unconscious bias. C, empowerment.

79
00:06:10.017 --> 00:06:13.433
The answer is B: unconscious bias.

80
00:06:14.917 --> 00:06:19.052
According to the text, what tends to reduce disparities?

81
00:06:19.352 --> 00:06:26.802
A, transparent pay equity reviews. B, longer working hours. C, fewer women in leadership.

82
00:06:30.817 --> 00:06:34.651
The answer is A: transparent pay equity reviews.

83
00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:42.158
Now it's your turn. Part one: What can be done to achieve gender equality in the workplace?

84
00:06:47.458 --> 00:06:53.683
Part two: Answer in a formal register: give two measures and justify each.

85
00:06:59.983 --> 00:07:13.804
Firstly, organisations should conduct transparent pay equity reviews, as these reveal hidden disparities. Secondly, flexible work-life policies would enable parents of both genders to pursue their careers.

86
00:07:14.104 --> 00:07:16.557
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:17.550 --> 00:07:21.874
Great work today! You learned eight new words and register and tone.

88
00:07:22.174 --> 00:07:25.767
Today's value is respect. Thank the people who help us.

89
00:07:26.067 --> 00:07:29.311
See you in Unit twenty-one. Goodbye!

90
00:07:29.611 --> 00:07:32.136
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

